## What If You Are Looking for Therapy for Anxiety or Depression?

People often begin searching for a therapist after noticing that stress, worry, low mood, or other emotional concerns are affecting their daily lives.

Medicare identifies outpatient mental health services as including care used to diagnose and treat mental health conditions such as depression and anxiety.

You don't need to determine your own diagnosis before contacting a mental health professional.

A qualified provider can listen to your concerns, discuss what you are experiencing, and help determine whether counseling may be appropriate.

Therapy may focus on areas such as:

- Managing stress
- Understanding patterns of anxiety
- Coping with life transitions
- Improving relationships
- Processing grief
- Building healthier coping strategies
- Managing emotional difficulties
- Addressing trauma-related concerns

The appropriate approach depends on the individual and their goals.

## What If You Are Having Trouble Finding an In-Network Therapist?

Sometimes finding a therapist who meets both your clinical needs and your insurance requirements takes time.

If your first choice is unavailable, consider asking your insurance company for additional behavioral health providers.

You can also ask a counseling practice whether another therapist within the practice participates in your plan.

If you cannot find a suitable in-network provider, ask your HealthSpring plan about its out-of-network rules and whether there are circumstances under which out-of-network behavioral health care may be covered.

Don't assume that you have no options simply because one provider is unavailable.

## Why Insurance Verification Matters

Insurance verification can help reduce surprises, but it is important to understand that a benefits check is not the same as a guarantee of payment.

Your insurance company ultimately determines coverage according to your plan's terms, eligibility, network status, and applicable requirements.

That's why it is helpful to keep your insurance information current and notify the provider if your plan changes.

If you switch Medicare Advantage plans, don't assume that your previous therapist will automatically remain in-network.

Verify again.

## When Professional Mental Health Support May Be Helpful

There are many reasons someone might consider therapy.

You may want professional support if you are experiencing ongoing difficulties with:

- Anxiety or excessive worry
- Depression or changes in mood
- Grief and loss
- Relationship concerns
- Family conflict
- Major life transitions
- Stress
- Trauma-related concerns
- Loneliness or isolation
- Difficulty coping with changes in daily life

You do not have to wait for a situation to become a crisis before talking with a mental health professional.

A therapist can help you explore your concerns and identify appropriate goals for counseling.

If you are experiencing an immediate mental health crisis or are concerned about your safety, contact emergency services or call/text **988** for the Suicide & Crisis Lifeline. Medicare also directs people experiencing an immediate medical crisis to call 911.

